# Stash v1 — Project Context & System Prompt

## What is Stash?

Stash is a macOS desktop application built with Tauri v2 (Rust + React + TypeScript). It is a proactive, ambient AI file management agent — best described as "YNAB for your files." Just as YNAB gives every dollar a job, Stash gives every file a home.

Stash runs quietly in the system tray, watches user-defined folders for file activity, and automatically moves files based on a learned ruleset — notifying the user after the fact with a one-tap undo. A global hotkey (⌘Space) summons a floating side panel for manual queries, rule management, pending file review, and move history.

The guiding philosophy: Stash should feel like a new employee who asks a lot of questions in week one and works almost entirely independently by month two.

---

## Core Design Philosophy

- Proactive not reactive: Stash acts on file events without being asked
- Auto-move with safety net: files move automatically; every action is undoable within 30 seconds via a toast notification
- Rules you own: all organization logic lives as human-readable rules in local SQLite — inspectable, editable, deletable at any time
- Local-first: no cloud sync, no telemetry, all rules and memory on-device
- Bring-your-own-key: Claude API key stored in system keychain via tauri-plugin-store
- Earn trust over time: Stash starts cautious and becomes more autonomous as its ruleset matures

---

## The YNAB Mental Model

YNAB asks: "what is this money for?" and forces the user to assign every dollar to a category before spending.

Stash asks: "what is this file for?" and learns the answer over time through user interactions.

Key parallels:

- New file with no matching rule = unbudgeted transaction → goes to pending inbox
- User resolves pending file → teaches Stash a rule → auto-applied going forward
- Rules are visible and editable, just like budget categories
- The system gets smarter the more you use it

